## Chapter 23: The Weight of Rank 12
The corridor leading to the lower tactical bay was packed with students, but the usual chaotic noise of Anvil Ridge was noticeably muted. A strange tension hung in the air—a mix of quiet disbelief and suppressed resentment.
Kaelen slouched through the heavy double doors, his hands tucked deep into his frayed jacket pockets. Beneath his sleeves and inside his boots, the newly forged Kinetic-Void bracers and Anchor-Greaves sat flush and silent against his skin, adding an invisible layer of lethal readiness to his weak-looking frame.
Near the entrance pillars of the assembly hall, Renna, Tobias, and Mina were standing in a tight circle.
Renna looked immaculate—her dark hair neatly cropped, her uniform brushed, and her dented tower shield strapped securely to her back. Tobias was shifting his weight nervously from foot to foot, adjusting the leather strap of his unloaded crossbow, while Mina leaned against a concrete pillar, her sharp eyes scanning every student who passed by.
"There he is," Mina said softly, nodding toward Kaelen as he approached.
Renna turned, a genuine, warm smile breaking across her face. "Kaelen! Did you finish filing the salvage manifest with Silas?"
"Yeah," Kaelen stammered meekly, lowering his eyes and rubbing the back of his neck with a dirty palm. "Silas... Silas took the paperwork. He said we're cleared on the Vault 3 inventory."
"Good," Renna said, her tone turning serious as she motioned for them to step closer into the shadow of the pillar, away from prying ears. "Because things upstairs are moving a lot faster than we expected."
Tobias leaned in, his voice hushed and anxious. "Man, the rumor mill is spinning out of control. I just saw two transport cars from **Aether-Dynamics** and **Iron-Clad Security** park in the VIP staging lot. Corporate scouts don't visit Anvil Ridge unless they're looking to sign cheap labor."
"They aren't here for cheap labor this time," Mina corrected, her voice dropping into a low hiss. "I overheard Instructor Horgan talking to the municipal liaison near the stairs. Because our squad and Squad 2 finished in the top fifteen, the city is forcing a **Mandatory Draft Allocation**. We're going to be offered official guild contracts."
Tobias’s eyes went wide. "Official contracts? Like... real Guild Scout offers?"
"Don't celebrate yet, Tobias," Renna warned, her jaw tight as her amber eyes flicked toward the upper balcony where several well-dressed corporate representatives were looking down at the crowd. "Rank 12 sounds incredible on paper, but it puts a target on our backs. Corporate guilds don't like when auxiliary students out-score their sponsored cadets. Leon Thorne’s family is already calling our performance an 'anomaly caused by environmental instability.'"
Kaelen kept his posture slightly stooped, putting on an expression of mild panic. "Are... are they going to revoke our score?"
"They can't revoke a registered System Tag," Renna said firmly, placing a reassuring hand on Kaelen’s shoulder. "The Defense Bureau logged the Behemoth tag officially. But what they *can* do is pressure us during the Guild Draft interviews. They’ll try to split our squad up—offer Tobias and Mina low-tier scout contracts, offer me a frontline vanguard spot, and..." She hesitated, looking at Kaelen with a mix of pity and fierce protectiveness.
"And leave me behind?" Kaelen completed softly, lowering his head.
"They'll try to drop you back into the auxiliary pool because you're a Salvager with no registered combat skills," Renna said, her voice turning hard as iron. "They think you're dead weight we dragged across the finish line."
She looked at Tobias, then at Mina, before making eye contact with Kaelen.
"I called us together before we go into that hall because I want to make one thing clear," Renna said, her voice ringing with absolute conviction. "We entered Sector 4 as Squad 4, and we survive as Squad 4. If a Guild Scout tries to sign me or Tobias or Mina, the deal only stands if they take all four of us—including our Salvager. We don't sell out the people who held our line."
Tobias nodded eagerly. "Damn right. Kaelen pulled your vest during that Behemoth charge, Renna. We stick together."
Mina twirled a dull training dagger between her fingers, a rare, faint smile touching her lips. "Agreed. Besides, Kaelen's weird luck keeps us alive."
Kaelen looked at the three of them, letting a breathless, relieved smile cross his face. "I... thank you, guys. Really."
In the quiet dark behind his eyes, Kaelen’s cold calculations adjusted instantly.
*Having a loyal, permanent squad cover isn't just nice—it's tactical perfection,* he reasoned. *If Renna's squad remains locked as a unit, corporate recruiters won't be able to isolate me for individual stat audits. I can continue operating behind her shield while we take higher-tier Guild contracts.*
Suddenly, the heavy iron bell above the tactical bay rang three times—a deafening *CLANG-CLANG-CLANG* that echoed down the concrete corridor.
"Assembly is starting," Renna said, taking a deep breath and slinging her tower shield higher on her shoulder. "Keep your heads up. Whatever Thorne or the corporate scouts throw at us in there, we hold our ground."
As Renna led the squad through the heavy doors into the crowded assembly hall, Kaelen fell into step behind her right shoulder, pulling his worn hood slightly lower over his brow.
Through the double doors ahead, Kaelen’s **[Low-Light Spatial Perception]** and **[Hive-Mind Synapse]** instantly picked up the heavy, suffocating pressure radiating from the front row.
Sitting in the VIP section, flanked by armed corporate guards and Tier-1 Guild representatives, was **Leon Thorne**. His right arm was wrapped in heavy medical bracing, his face pale and furious, his dark eyes fixed directly on the entrance—waiting for Anvil Ridge's "lucky" squad to step into the light.
Kaelen’s fingers twitched subtly under his sleeves, his new Kinetic-Void bracers humming with cold, hidden power.
